<h2 id="faq" style="color:#0f172a;font-size:24px;font-weight:700 !important;line-height:1.3;margin-top:48px;margin-bottom:16px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3 style="color:#0f172a;font-size:20px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.3;margin-top:32px;margin-bottom:12px;">Is Florida's housing market going to crash?</h3>

<p style="font-size:18px;line-height:1.8;color:#374151;margin-bottom:20px;"><strong style="color:#0f172a;">Unlikely.</strong> Unlike 2008, Florida has continued in-migration (100K+ annually), job growth in healthcare, tech, and logistics, stricter lending standards (fewer risky mortgages), and inventory still below long-term normal (6-8 months is "balanced"). What could cause a correction: major hurricane causing mass insurance cancellations, sustained mortgage rates above 8%, national recession plus job losses, or exodus due to climate/insurance costs. Most likely scenario is flat to modest growth for 2-3 years, not a crash.</p>

<h3 style="color:#0f172a;font-size:20px;font-weight:700;line-height:1.3;margin-top:32px;margin-bottom:12px;">When will Florida home prices drop?</h3>

<p style="font-size:18px;line-height:1.8;color:#374151;margin-bottom:20px;">Prices already dropped 1-2% in 2026 in many markets. Forecasts for 2026: Tampa -1 to -2%, Miami +1 to +2% (luxury strength), Orlando -1.5%, Jacksonville -1.5%. Bigger drops (5-10%) are unlikely unless rates spike to 8%+, a major economic shock hits, or the insurance market collapses. Waiting for prices to fall 10-15% means missing 2-3 years of principal paydown and risking rates dropping (which would push prices back up).</p>

